# Curving the Integer Line

Begin with the central construction: the Cayley transform sends the translated point $n+i$ to $p_n=n/(n+2i)$ inside the Poincare disk. Drag the endpoint and see the infinite arithmetic line begin to bend toward the ideal boundary.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The accompanying executable table exposes the exact rational radius and defect rather than relying only on the picture.

{{demo:0}}

## Exact geometry at a glance

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the exact radius derivation</summary>

Multiplying $n/(n+2i)$ by $(n-2i)/(n-2i)$ gives real part $n^2/(n^2+4)$ and imaginary part $-2n/(n^2+4)$. Therefore $|p_n|^2=n^2/(n^2+4)$ and $1-|p_n|^2=4/(n^2+4)$.

</details>

{{algorithm:0}}

## The same approach to the boundary, measured two ways

Euclidean radius approaches one, but hyperbolic distance grows without bound. Explore both quantities side by side, then inspect their graphs.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the intrinsic-distance formula</summary>

The disk distance from zero is $2\operatorname{artanh}|p_n|$. Substituting $|p_n|=|n|/\sqrt{n^2+4}$ yields $2\operatorname{arsinh}(|n|/2)$.

</details>

## Exact finite counting

The radius through $p_N$ contains precisely the indices with $|n|\le N$, so it contains $2N+1$ distinct points. No floating-point comparison is needed.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

